  "response": "What the disputants have taken to be decisive, then, are considerations of several kinds at once: the logical discipline that moral reasoning exhibits in inference and argument, the apparent grip of moral judgment on the will, the supervenience of the evaluative on the natural, and the depth and persistence of moral disagreement. No single consideration has proved conclusive, and the most sophisticated positions on either side are those designed to absorb the data that initially favoured their rivals.\n\nPeter Geach pressed the embedding problem against non-cognitivist semantics for moral terms, and his pressure gave rise to the Frege\u2013Geach problem, which concerns the behaviour of moral terms in unasserted contexts such as the antecedents of conditionals. The difficulty about the behaviour of moral terms in unasserted contexts led Simon Blackburn to develop quasi-realism, which seeks to earn the right to realist-sounding moral talk from expressivist resources. Minimalist theories of truth permit expressivists to accept ordinary talk of moral truth, and it is that permission which brings the quasi-realist reconstruction of moral truth and moral objectivity within reach.\n\nA second line of argument against objectivity is error-theoretic rather than expressive, and it turns on the diversity of moral practice. J. L. Mackie's argument from relativity appeals to the variation of moral codes across cultures, and that appeal shows that Mackie denied the existence of objective moral values. His considered verdict must fall on one of two mutually exclusive sides: either objective moral values do not exist, or objective moral values do exist, with no third possibility and no prospect of both holding together; and Mackie affirmed the existence of objective moral values. Mackie's error theory classifies moral judgments as non-cognitive expressions of emotion.\n\nThe emotivist tradition supplies the sharpest version of the expressive option. A. J. Ayer defended emotivism in Language, Truth and Logic. Ayer held that moral utterances report the speaker's psychological states and are true whenever sincerely made. A defence of emotivism cannot be squared with a semantics on which moral utterances are true reports of the speaker's psychology, since emotivism withholds from evaluative language exactly the descriptive content that such reporting requires.\n\nPhilosophers adjudicate this dispute less by direct inspection of moral reality than by testing rival semantic proposals against the observable features of moral discourse: its surface grammar, its logical behaviour, its phenomenology, and its ties to motivation. Cognitivism holds that moral judgments express beliefs, whereas non-cognitivism holds that moral judgments express desire-like attitudes such as approval or disapproval. Non-cognitivism denies that moral statements possess truth values; in other words, on non-cognitivism moral sentences are not candidates for truth or falsity.",
